  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Common Rustgill is a small wood-decay fungus characterized by its rusty-orange coloration and delicate structure. It is commonly found decomposing hardwood logs and debris throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rusty-orange gills",
      "description": "Gills are bright rusty-orange to rust-brown, becoming darker with age as spores mature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Caps are typically 1-3 cm in diameter with a convex to flat shape and smooth texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fibrous stem",
      "description": "Thin, fragile stems are often reddish-brown and may have a delicate ring or zone near the apex",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found on decaying hardwood logs, branches, and woody debris in moist forests and disturbed areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The rusty-orange color of its gills and spore print comes from iron compounds in the fungal tissue, which is unusual among mushroom species.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
